.TH brother-td-app 1 "2026-11-17" "brother-td-cups 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
brother-td-app \- PAPPL printer application for Brother TD-4D label printers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brother-td-app
.RB [ \-\-device " uri" ]
.RB [ \-\-model " model" ]
.RB [ \-\-printer " name" ]
.RB [ \-\-port " port" ]
.RB [ \-\-spool " directory" ]
.RB [ \-\-log " file" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B brother-td-app
is a PAPPL-based IPP Everywhere printer application for Brother TD-4D
label printers.
It exposes the printer without a PPD, so it can be used with CUPS 3,
Android, iOS/iPadOS (AirPrint), and other IPP Everywhere clients.
It advertises the
.B _universal
DNS-SD subtype used by AirPrint and is marked as Mopria-certified.
.PP
The application accepts PWG and Apple raster jobs directly and uses
Ghostscript to convert PDF and PostScript jobs.
.PP
The common label sizes are advertised as separate virtual media sources
because AirPrint builds its paper-size menu from the ready media of each
source.  Selecting a virtual roll selects the page size; it does not require
different hardware.
.PP
Android's built-in and Mopria print services ignore custom media names and
only display a fixed list of standard PWG sizes.  The application therefore
also advertises standard sizes such as
.BR oe_photo-l_3.5x5in ,
.BR om_card_54x86mm ,
.BR om_dsc-photo_89x119mm ,
and
.BR jpn_hagaki_100x148mm .
Custom sizes such as 4x5 and 4x3 remain available to AirPrint, CUPS, and
other IPP clients.
.PP
The application also serves an ASCII printer-strings catalog.  This prevents
CUPS/libppd from writing the UTF-8 double-prime used by its built-in inch
localizations into a PPD declared as ISO-8859-1, which makes labels such as
.B 4 x 6
appear garbled in some print dialogs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-device " uri"
Create one printer for the given PAPPL device URI, for example
.B file:/tmp/capture
or
.BR socket://192.168.1.50:9100 .
.TP
.BI \-\-model " model"
Model name for
.BR \-\-device ,
for example
.BR TD-4410D .
.TP
.BI \-\-printer " name"
Printer name for
.BR \-\-device .
The model name is used by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
IPP port.  The default is
.BR auto :
the application chooses a free port on first start, saves it in
.IR SPOOL/port ,
and reuses it on later starts.  Use
.B 0
or
.B auto
to request this explicitly, or a port number to pin the port.
.TP
.BI \-\-spool " directory"
Spool directory. The default is
.I /var/spool/brother-td-app
when running as root, otherwise
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/brother-td-app
or
.IR ~/.local/state/brother-td-app .
The directory is created automatically.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file"
Log file. The default is standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " file"
State file used to persist printers between runs. The default is
.IR SPOOL/state .
.TP
.B \-\-no-state
Do not load or save printer state. Use this if an old state file keeps
recreating a stale printer configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable PAPPL debug logging.
.TP
.B \-\-check-device
Open the URI given with
.B \-\-device
and exit. This is useful for checking USB permissions and device URIs.
.TP
.B \-\-no-tls
Do not advertise or require IPPS/TLS. Use this if an Android, iOS, or other
driverless client discovers the printer but cannot connect to the
application's self-signed certificate. The application then advertises only
unencrypted IPP.  The packaged service enables this option by default; remove
it from the service command to enable IPPS.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a printer application with a local USB printer:
.PP
.nf
brother-td-app --model TD-4410D \\
    --device 'usb://Brother/TD-4410D?serial=...'
.fi
.PP
Run a test instance that writes to a file:
.PP
.nf
brother-td-app --model TD-4410D --device file:/tmp/td.out \\
    --spool /tmp/td-spool
.fi
.PP
Use
.B \-\-port 8000
if you want a fixed IPP port instead of the automatically selected one.
.PP
Then add it to CUPS as an IPP Everywhere printer.  The selected port is saved
in
.IR SPOOL/port :
.PP
.nf
PORT=$(cat /tmp/td-spool/port)
sudo lpadmin -p TD4410 -E \\
    -v "ipp://localhost:$PORT/ipp/print/TD4410" -m everywhere
lp -d TD4410 -o media=76x127mm label.pdf
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR brother-td (7),
.BR brother-td-filter (7),
.BR pappl (7)
